{"name":"Beaujolais","hex":"#80304c","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Beaujolais","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A historical province of France known for winemaking, covering parts of Rhône-Alpes and Burgundy."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A light-bodied French red wine made from Gamay grapes."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Beaujolais"}],"entries":[{"title":"Beaujolais","blocks":[{"text":"Beaujolais is a French appellation d'origine contrôlée (AOC) wine in the Burgundy region. Red Beaujolais wines are generally made of the Gamay grape, which has a thin skin and is low in tannin, but like most AOC wines they are not labeled varietally. Whites, 1% of production, are made mostly with Chardonnay, though Aligoté was also permitted until 2024. Beaujolais tends to be a very light-bodied red wine, with relatively high acidity. In some vintages, Beaujolais produces more wine than the Burgundy wine regions of Chablis, Côte d'Or, Côte Chalonnaise, and Mâconnais put together."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Carte_vignoble_beaujolais.png/330px-Carte_vignoble_beaujolais.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beaujolais","wikidata":"Q616"},{"title":"Beaujolais (province)","blocks":[{"text":"Beaujolais is a historical province and wine-producing region in France. It is located north of Lyon, and covers parts of the departments of Rhône and Saône-et-Loire. The region is known internationally for its long tradition of winemaking, and more recently for the Beaujolais nouveau."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beaujolais_(province)","wikidata":"Q7179060"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/16th-century_unknown_painters_-_Philip_the_Bold_-_WGA23677.jpg/500px-16th-century_unknown_painters_-_Philip_the_Bold_-_WGA23677.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=p